{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Long-Term Memory.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Long-Term Memory.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"long term memory long term memory","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Long-Term Memory.md","fileName":"Long-Term Memory.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Long-Term Memory","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 13:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Human Cognition]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human Cognition","Working Memory","Skill Acquisition","Navigation","Cognitive Map","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Long-Term Memory.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Long-Term Memory.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"long term memory long term memory","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Long-Term Memory.md","fileName":"Long-Term Memory.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Long-Term Memory","heading":"Long-Term Memory","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Long-term memory stores information about the world, concepts, events, and procedures beyond the temporary capacity of [[Working Memory]]. Declarative memory holds facts and events, while nondeclarative memory includes priming, skills, habits, conditioning, and habituation.\n\nRecall reactivates memory traces and is influenced by cues and context. It can also be biased, misattributed, blocked, or altered by suggestion. Interfaces can support recall by providing meaningful cues and consistent mappings, while repeated interaction can turn explicit procedures into skills through [[Skill Acquisition]].\n\nSpatial information accumulated during [[Navigation]] is represented by a [[Cognitive Map]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human Cognition","Working Memory","Skill Acquisition","Navigation","Cognitive Map","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf"],"properties":[],"p